{"id": "da4ed58d-2b51-4663-ab1b-d59d425d828d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This is a broad EBS readiness review across an account in us-east-1, so I should delegate to a sub-agent rather than digging through it myself token-inefficiently. I need to figure out which accounts are in scope, possibly letting the sub-agent enumerate associations itself, and I'll let the user know my plan before proceeding.

I'll split the work into two read-only sub-agents: one covering configuration (volumes, encryption, snapshots, DLM/AWS Backup, unattached volumes) and another covering performance and monitoring (CloudWatch metrics, alarms, IOPS throttling, volume types), keeping the review shallow by default. I'll draft a short message to the user, then kick off both sub-agents.

"}]}

{"id": "9a44a571-9f7c-4755-9102-62e67fa2aaea", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"text": "I'll review your EBS setup in us-east-1 from two angles. One pass covers configuration: encryption, snapshots and backups, volume types, and unattached or stale volumes. The other covers performance and monitoring: utilization, throttling, and alarm coverage. Then I'll give you a readiness verdict.", "type": "text"}, {"id": "tooluse_GZogzruAohUKhF8bn0vBSa", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-config-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Read-only production-readiness review of the EBS configuration in us-east-1. Do not make any changes.",
  "context": "The user has a launch next week and wants to know if their EBS storage is production-ready. This slice covers configuration and data protection only. Another reviewer covers performance metrics and alarms, so skip those.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 012345678901_1 (agent space account); also check other associated accounts if list_associations shows any", "Region us-east-1"],
  "criteria": "Inventory EBS volumes (ec2 describe_volumes) and attachments to instances. Check: (1) encryption status and KMS key type per volume, plus whether account-level default EBS encryption is enabled (get_ebs_encryption_by_default); (2) snapshot coverage: most recent snapshot age per in-use volume, plus Data Lifecycle Manager policies and AWS Backup plans or protected resources that cover EBS; (3) volume types, e.g. legacy gp2/io1/standard where gp3/io2 would fit, and IOPS/throughput settings; (4) unattached ('available') volumes, volumes with DeleteOnTermination set on non-root volumes, and single-AZ or no-redundancy concerns for critical data; (5) snapshot sharing/public exposure, and fast snapshot restore if relevant; (6) tagging hygiene (Name, Environment, Owner). Return a concise summary with counts, a list of specific volume IDs with issues, and a severity rating (critical, high, medium or low) for each finding. Keep it shallow: report findings and suggest areas for a deeper look."
}"}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS configuration and backups in us-east-1"}, {"id": "tooluse_DPkKdFHEPfXczRaWQAzytn", "tool_name": "subagent", "input": {"name": "ebs-perf-monitoring-review", "agent_type": "generic", "prompt": "{
  "task": "Read-only review of EBS performance headroom and monitoring/alarm coverage in us-east-1. Do not make any changes.",
  "context": "The user has a launch next week and wants to know if their EBS storage is production-ready. This slice covers performance and observability only. Another reviewer covers encryption, backups and volume config, so skip those.",
  "resources": ["AWS account 012345678901_1 (agent space account); also check other associated accounts if list_associations shows any", "Region us-east-1", "CloudWatch namespaces AWS/EBS and AWS/EC2"],
  "timeframe": "Last 14 days, ending 2026-11-23T17:00:00Z",
  "criteria": "For in-use volumes, use CloudWatch AWS/EBS metrics (VolumeReadOps, VolumeWriteOps, VolumeReadBytes, VolumeWriteBytes, VolumeQueueLength, VolumeIdleTime, BurstBalance for gp2/st1/sc1, and VolumeThroughputPercentage/VolumeConsumedReadWriteOps for io1/io2 where applicable) to find: volumes whose peak IOPS or throughput approaches provisioned limits, gp2 volumes with low or depleting BurstBalance, high queue lengths, and instance-level EBS bandwidth/IOPS limits being hit (EC2 EBSIOBalance%/EBSByteBalance% for burstable instance types). Also check EBS-related CloudWatch alarms (describe_alarms): which volumes lack alarms on BurstBalance, VolumeQueueLength, or status checks (describe_volume_status for impaired volumes). Return a concise summary with specific volume IDs, peak values vs limits, and severity (critical, high, medium or low) for each finding. Keep it shallow: report findings and suggest areas for a deeper look."
}", "preloaded_skills": ["exploring-metrics"]}, "type": "tool_use", "display_intent": "Review EBS performance headroom and alarms in us-east-1"}], "created_at": null}

{"id": "6eb2f6e8-5be5-4d51-b755-df3bbb77aa9e", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice vol-1234567890abcdef0_11 has a severely-degraded io-performance event flagged, and checking vol-1234567890abcdef0_3, its gp3 IOPS-to-size ratio is right at the 500 IOPS/GiB max, which is fine. Next I should pull the metrics and also check the instance types involved.

